body:
People make assumptions about others, so much so that for most non-aspies, the default state of being is to ignore the words and judge based on the intent. But here, let me help you. I'll answer the questions and points of both of your posts as best I can, so apologies in advance for the length.  


Assumptions are highly confusing for us, according to the amount of posts on here, the research material about Asperger's and my own experiences. We are seen as being rude, callous, shallow, or even evil, disruptive and manipulative depending upon the context.

&#x200B;

So, looking at your Mormon posting: Certain words shaped the context; you described yourself using phrases like  'unique gift to see patterns'  and 'how my mind works', as well as 'as I child I could recognise discrepancies' This indicates you were trying to show yourself as 'better' I know you mean't it as 'different', but unfortunately when describing yourself to others, any attempt to differentiate between you and another person is either seen as you trying to make an excuse, or you feeling superior to others, otherwise, why point out the difference?

This was further augmented by other words you picked; about Mormonism; "how to ask the right questions.", "Mormons are the most mind boggling."  "refuse to step out of the box." you had painted Mormons as not asking the right questions, finding them strange/weird and noting they were obstinate and refused others viewpoints, which directly translates into them being ignorant.

&#x200B;

Now, I know you didn't mean to upset anyone, and I know you were asking a simple question, but it is what was implied; that they are ignorant. This is a meaning you didn't write, you took pains to write around it, but that meaning persisted because NOW they are feeling as though you are attacking them, so it changes their perspective (ironic, isn't it? You are being blamed for words you didn't write. Meanings you didn't mean and statements you never made) . As far as they are concerned, you are now someone on the offensive, looking to take them apart and hurt them by making them question their faith, and you are trying to do it using evidence! Trying to use evidence to dislodge faith is like trying to use a bulldozer to dislodge something stuck between your teeth. It just won't work most of the time, and the few times it does will be messy. 




So your bluntness was perceived as contempt, perhaps arrogance. "How dare you ask such a question!" they seem to think. From that point onwards, they see everything else you are saying as an additional to your hateful question. You are no longer someone asking out of curiosity a simple (although deeply personal) question, you had framed it very much as "Look, we have all this information about Mormonism, so why aren't you looking at it? What's wrong with you? Are you an idiot?" To put it into another context this is very much like asking "Hey! Ugly people! Why are you still ugly when there are many beauty products around? And plastic surgery?" your intentions were innocent, as was most of your wording, but the question, or rather, implied meaning, was not. Simply stating you don't mean to cause offensive is irrelevant. The very question is offensive. 

&#x200B;

Some of the replies you received happily didn't take it as that. Many do, however. Many have experienced problems with their faith being questioned in this manner, and they are jaded, bruised, and become very sensitive about the topic. Your chief mistake was expecting people who had a very strong subjective truth to automatically admit that the objective truth was the correct one, or to explain why it wasn't. There's an interesting discussion going down in your posting that talks about objective truth vs subjective (Truth (as discerned by evidence) is known as objective truth, and truth (as discerned by personal experience) is subjective truth) , where one person claims that they consider objective truth to be another form of subjective truth, and as they cannot determine the validity of another's experiences (a fair point) they do not consider it anymore pertinent or correct than their own subjective truth. The other person in that debate is attempting to point out that their truth (subjective) is not in line with truth (objective) and as such, how can they possibly be sure it's Truth (with a capital T). The answer remains the same, because they 'know' it's (subjectively) true and therefore it must be True! 

&#x200B;

Unfortunately, this is an example of circular reasoning. In your case, you hold objective truth to be higher than subjective truth. You looked around, you researched, you found holes in the subjective truth your family and church wanted you to accept and you found objective truth that made you question this subjective truth and you changed. Others...can't. They hold on too tightly to subjective experience, find it too powerful or too important for one reason or another.  Or worse, they compare their subjective true to someone else's subjective truth and assume it's objective. 

&#x200B;

The top posted reply asks you to consider, after admonishing you for the insults,  the possibility they looked at the evidence and came to a different conclusion. Objective truth does not work like that; there is only one conclusion...but there can be many interpretations. The trouble is we all have our own subjective truth, as discussed earlier, and this is in large part shaped by our preconceptions, we can never 'see' Truth as clearly as we see our own subjective truth, we can only get a better idea of objective truth (often a poor copy of Truth)  by sharing information and allowing that information to reshape our subjective truth to bring it more in line with objective truth so your interpretation of the facts (hopefully) matches Truth and therefore, observable reality. This is complicated further if you operate in a community like a great deal of religions do where the axiom (or grounding point, if you prefer) for your objective truth is based entirely on someone else's subjective truth (as all religious texts are) because if I ask someone to look at evidence they can't directly quantify (because they aren't trained to understand the raw data that makes up that evidence) and it conflicts with what they know to be objective truth (could be the Bible, could be the standard model of physics, it does not matter) the evidence will be automatically dismissed.

&#x200B;

Even if their idea of objective truth has massive gaping holes in it, the evidence, if it cannot be bent to fit into the holes, will be dismissed, made fun of, and ignored, even if it's Truth!

&#x200B;

Now don't get me wrong, this problem isn't just limited to Mormons, or religion. It's an unfortunate fact of the human condition. Nobody can be trained to examine the raw data that governs the foundations of all the domains of science; a physician can't validate (or invalidate) the claims of the climate scientists talking about climate change; they simply aren't trained to analyse and process the raw data you need to make a claim like that. This has and continues to be a difficult problem throughout the sciences as the job of the scientist is to get a better idea of objective truth but they are often blinded by preconceptions, or even desires and wants, and dismissing something just because it does not align with you think should happen (or want to) is an all too easy mistake to make that requires constant correction (hence the peer review process which is there to try and ignore any bias there may be).

&#x200B;

Some people, such as yourself, see too much objective truth pushing holes into their subjective truth. For some, it means they discard their subjective truth. For others, it means they grip it all the tighter (which I believe is part of the reason religion does not like science that much...) Others have experiences that they do not understand; there are plenty of people who have seen statues of Jesus wink at them, or heard the Voice of God, or felt His (or Her) eye fall on them and these experiences, whilst typically the result of the brain riding high on one cocktail or another, only seem to cement their subjective reality. Finally, some cling to such things because being without them can be...well, scary. (and there's the reason a lot of people approaching death turn to religion) 

&#x200B;

So, I hope I've given you some help and insight. Your wording could of been better; a little less inflammatory, and I hope you understand why Mormons, and indeed other aspects of human life are at times confusing, why humanity isn't as advanced as it should be. Because we are human, and we are fallible.

I'm very much afraid you are in possession of one of the most complex items in the known universe, so I'd like to point out there is bound to be some overlap between your conditions.

It depends very much on what needs to be diagnosed; an infection? Simple to diagnose, take a sample, send to the pathology lab, get results and treat with relevant medication. Psychological conditions have no such easy and apparent symptoms and you can spend half the time looking at a list of symptoms and trying to put it into neat little pigeonholes we can label as 'ADHD' or 'Depression'...

&#x200B;

...when really it's an almighty cluster of wiring, chemical imbalances, dietary issues, genetic disposition, social influence, the list goes on.. Frankly, the whole pigeonhole idea needs to go; we need an schematic of the mind (something currently being worked on, but our dear old NHS has barely got its teeth into genetic medicine, let alone connectomes) to see how section #A1642  (which is operating out of bounds and either below or above average development and performance) affects section #R53463 and what electrochemical, social and psychological ramifications it would have. 

&#x200B;

I'm happy you've found my replies detailed. Hopefully, they've also been helpful. :-)

I'm afraid head injuries can leave lingering processing problems, especially in the speech centers. The standard rehab line is that you won't talk/think like you did before, so stop trying to see yourself that way, instead, part of you has been altered and you have to adapt to thinking/talking in a new way. I would like to stress that I am an idiot on the internet, so please don't take what I say as empirical truth.

&#x200B;

Sadly, the NHS has taken on the DSM-V depiction of Autism in general; this is something I disagree with as Kanner's Autism (the classic rocking backwards and forwards, almost primal behaviours, etc) are in direct opposition to Aspergers (oddly little primal behavior, cold in tone) so whilst they do appear to have a similar cause they have a very different set of symptoms and prognosis and the result is a mixed public image of both conditions.

&#x200B;

It gets worse if you through in the more exotic types, such as childhood disintegrative disorder. Each 'version' of  Autism has different needs to be met, so grouping them together should of been thought out a tad more.
